  'P0300': ['Random Misfire Detected\nPossible causes: Ignition system fault; Fuel delivery fault; Misaligned/Damaged CKP sensor trigger wheel; Engine mechanical fault; Running out of fuel; Evaporative Emission System; Fuel Quality Note: More than one cylinder misfire or misfiring cylinder could not be identified due to missing camshaft position (CMP) sensor signal. The Misfire Detection monitor is an on-board strategy designed to monitor engine misfire and identify the specific cylinder in which the misfire has occurred. Misfire is defined as lack of combustion in a cylinder due to absence of spark, poor fuel metering, poor compression, or any other cause', 'Multiple cylinder misfire detected', 'Random/Multiple Cylinder Misfire Detected'],
  'P0301': ['Cylinder 1 Misfire Detected\nPossible causes: Broken Compression Rings; Inoperative Injector Assembly; Leaking or Bent Valve; Bent Push Rod; Broken Rocker Arm Bolt; Bent Connecting Rod', 'Random Misfire Detected', 'Cylinder 1misfire detected', 'Powertrain Control Module (PCM) Read Only Memory (ROM) test error\nPossible causes: Damaged PCM. Note: Equivalent to OBDII DTC P0605. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) tests the ROM during Self-Test by performing a \'checksum\' test on all ROM locations. The Diagnostic Trouble Code (DTC) is set if the value returned from the \'checksum\' test is less than or greater than zero, this indicates that the ROM has been corrupted', 'Future emission related code. No action is required'],
  'P0302': ['Cylinder 2 Misfire Detected\nPossible causes: Broken Compression Rings; Inoperative Injector Assembly; Leaking or Bent Valve; Bent Push Rod; Broken Rocker Arm Bolt; Bent Connecting Rod', 'Future emission related code. No action is required', 'EGR Flow Malfunction\nPossible causes: Open or short in EGR circuitry; Damaged EGR valve; Damaged EGR Back Pressure Transducer (BPT); Obstructed / leaking EGR passage; Damaged EGR / EVAP control solenoid; Damaged EGR temperature (EGRT) sensor; Damaged PCM Note: Equivalent to OBDII DTC P0400'],
  'P0303': ['Cylinder 3 Misfire Detected\nPossible causes: Broken Compression Rings; Inoperative Injector Assembly; Leaking or Bent Valve; Bent Push Rod; Broken Rocker Arm Bolt; Bent Connecting Rod', 'Heated Oxygen Sensor(HO2S) Circuit Malfunction Bank 1 Sensor 1\nPossible causes: Short/open HO2S circuit; Exhaust leak; Intake Air system; Fuel system; Ignition system; Damaged HO2S sensor. Note: Equivalent to OBDII DTC P0130', 'Cylinder 3misfire detected'],
  'P0304': ['Cylinder #4 Misfire Detected\nPossible causes: Broken Compression Rings; Inoperative Injector Assembly; Leaking or Bent Valve; Bent Push Rod; Broken Rocker Arm Bolt; Bent Connecting Rod', 'Knock Sensor Circuit Malfunction\nPossible causes: PCM not receiving the signal; Open or short in harness; Damaged Knock Sensor; Damaged PCM. Note: Equivalent to OBDII DTC P0325', 'Future emission related code. No action is required'],
  'P0305': ['Cylinder 5 Misfire Detected\nPossible causes: Broken Compression Rings; Inoperative Injector Assembly; Leaking or Bent Valve; Bent Push Rod; Broken Rocker Arm Bolt; Bent Connecting Rod', 'Cylinder No. 5 Misfire Detected', 'EGR Temperature (EGRT) Sensor circuit malfunction\nPossible causes: Open or short in EGRT circuitry; Damaged EGRT sensor; Damaged PCM Note: Equivalent to OBDII DTC P1401'],
  'P0306': ['Cylinder 6 Misfire Detected\nPossible causes: Broken Compression Rings; Inoperative Injector Assembly; Leaking or Bent Valve; Bent Push Rod; Broken Rocker Arm Bolt; Bent Connecting Rod', 'Cylinder No. 6 Misfire Detected', 'Excessive EGR Flow Detected\nPossible causes: Open or short in EGR circuitry; Damaged EGR valve; Damaged EGR Back Pressure Transducer (BPT); Obstructed / leaking EGR passage; Damaged EGR / EVAP control solenoid; Damaged EGR temperature (EGRT) sensor; Damaged PCM Note: Equivalent to OBDII DTC P0402'],
  'P0307': ['Cylinder #7 Misfire Detected', 'CKP sensor / wiring', 'Closed Loop Control Malfunction\nPossible causes: Short/open HO2S circuit; Exhaust leak; Intake Air system; Fuel system; Ignition system; Damaged HO2S sensor. Note: Equivalent to OBDII DTC P1148'],
  'P0308': ['Cylinder #8 Misfire Detected'],
  'P0309': ['Cylinder #9 Misfire Detected', 'Vent control valve stuck open\nPossible causes: Blocked rubber tube to EVAP canister vent control solenoid; EVAP canister vent control solenoid; EVAP control system pressure sensor; Vacuum cut valve; Water seperator; EVAP canister is saturated with water. Note: Equivalent ODBII DTC P1448'],
